WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.411
Idempotency: what is safe to retry.

2
00:00:02.691 --> 00:00:09.773
A model call with no tools is safe to retry in the sense that matters: running it twice changes nothing in the world.

3
00:00:10.053 --> 00:00:16.645
It still costs money twice, and because generation varies between runs, the retry can produce a different answer.

4
00:00:16.925 --> 00:00:20.979
That matters if you already showed the user the first one or wrote it somewhere.

5
00:00:21.259 --> 00:00:22.709
Side effects are different.

6
00:00:22.989 --> 00:00:32.141
If a pipeline calls the model, then sends an email, then records success, a crash between the last two steps means a retry sends a second email.

7
00:00:32.421 --> 00:00:44.005
The fix is idempotency: give each operation a key derived from what it is (the job id, the ticket id plus the action) and make the side effect check the key before acting.

8
00:00:44.285 --> 00:00:55.891
Stripe’s API documents the canonical version: clients send an idempotency key, and the server returns the saved result for a repeated key instead of performing the operation again.

9
00:00:56.171 --> 00:01:03.723
The AWS article puts the rule plainly: don’t retry side-effecting calls unless the API is idempotent.

10
00:01:04.003 --> 00:01:06.584
For LLM systems this means two things.

11
00:01:06.864 --> 00:01:12.347
Make every write at the end of a pipeline idempotent, as you did for write tools in Studio 1.

12
00:01:12.627 --> 00:01:24.488
And decide what a retried generation means for the user: in a stream that has already shown text, don’t silently restart with a different answer (the lab’s streaming endpoint reports an error instead).
